1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It may be the most important invention of all time.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
The history of civilization has turned on the wheel.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
And we have traveled as far as we have because of it.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Farming, fighting, traveling, trading, even showing up on time.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:24,000
It's all nearly impossible to do without the wheel.

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, on Modern Marvels.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,660
The invention of the wheel, if I may put it this way, invented a mind.

8
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:14,920
And in becoming inventors, we begin to think of ourselves differently.

9
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:21,260
Not simply imperiled by the facts of the world, but perhaps competing with the facts of the world,

10
00:01:21,261 --> 00:01:27,340
as in a contest, and a contest that we plausibly now might regard ourselves as winning.

11
00:01:27,341 --> 00:01:34,340
The simple, elegant, yet revolutionary machine called the wheel has literally transformed

12
00:01:34,341 --> 00:01:37,520
mankind.

13
00:01:37,521 --> 00:01:42,280
Not just in the way we navigate the challenges of our lives, but in the very way we perceive

14
00:01:42,281 --> 00:01:44,220
ourselves.

15
00:01:44,221 --> 00:01:49,200
Quite a responsibility for a tool so ubiquitous that we encounter hundreds in a day without

16
00:01:49,201 --> 00:01:51,080
even realizing it.

17
00:01:51,081 --> 00:01:56,280
So simple that we struggle to adequately define it.

18
00:01:56,281 --> 00:02:03,800
I think a wheel is any circular object, regardless of its width, that rolls.

19
00:02:03,801 --> 00:02:11,700
And that means it might have an axle to transmit its power to someplace else or to direct its power at a certain point.

20
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:15,940
Some say an axle is not a necessary component of a wheel.

21
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:23,860
You can imagine wheels in which the mechanical wheel is not linked to that central axis, for example, in a ball bearing.

22
00:02:23,861 --> 00:02:26,300
A ball bearing goes around, it makes a circular motion.

23
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:30,740
It fulfills our definition of a wheel, but it doesn't have an axle.

24
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:35,280
The wheel and axle is one of six basic machines,

25
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:38,940
human-made objects that are the basis of all devices

26
00:02:38,941 --> 00:02:42,020
that provide us with mechanical advantage over our environment.

27
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:47,880
The other five include the lever, the inclined plane,

28
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:53,020
the pulley, the wedge, and the screw.

29
00:02:53,860 --> 00:03:00,240
You know, it's interesting how some of these machines, it becomes a vague distinction between them.

30
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:02,200
One of my favorites is a pizza cutter.

31
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:05,740
You've got a wheel, the edge is a wedge, and the handle is a lever.

32
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:11,020
So you're using a lever, a wedge, and a wheel to cut your pizza.

33
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:20,420
Mechanical advantage is important because it allows a single person with limited force to accomplish an otherwise impossible task.

34
00:03:20,421 --> 00:03:25,740
Consider the wheeled machine known as a pulley.

35
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:31,340
Less force is required to achieve a given effect.

36
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:35,160
Now you have to apply the force over a much longer distance, and that's the key.

37
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:39,860
With a pulley, instead of just lifting up a 500-pound block, 10 feet, say,

38
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:44,020
you might have to pull 100 feet of rope, but you don't have to apply as much force,

39
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:45,900
and so eventually you get the job done.

40
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:48,000
That's mechanical advantage.

41
00:03:48,001 --> 00:03:52,000
Some have speculated that the earliest wheels were logs,

42
00:03:52,001 --> 00:03:55,000
used to transport heavy loads.

43
00:03:55,001 --> 00:04:00,000
I think it might be useful to make clear how little we know about that

44
00:04:00,001 --> 00:04:04,000
and to what extent we've made up stories.

45
00:04:04,001 --> 00:04:07,000
They're pretty much made up stories.

46
00:04:07,001 --> 00:04:13,000
The archaeological evidence for the very first wheel is pretty slim.

47
00:04:13,001 --> 00:04:17,000
Nevertheless, someone somewhere invented a wheel.

48
00:04:17,001 --> 00:04:22,940
and in doing so gained mechanical advantage because the wheel reduces friction.

49
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:28,180
Unlike a flat, wide surface such as a placket of wood, which generates a lot of friction,

50
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:34,400
or a sharp, narrow point, the wheel has a smooth, narrow, and continuous surface.

51
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:40,400
It reduces friction because it makes contact on a small, narrow, but endless plane.

52
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:43,820
Plus, the wheel can store energy.

53
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,640
Now that's a very practical consequence of the invention,

54
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:52,100
but there's a much more profound consequence.

55
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:55,340
There's a cognitive consequence.

56
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:00,060
He is now reconsidering the very idea of limitation

57
00:05:00,061 --> 00:05:03,600
because he now has concrete evidence

58
00:05:03,601 --> 00:05:08,280
that what he once took to be unquestionably impossible

59
00:05:08,281 --> 00:05:12,340
is now not only possible, but practically achieved.

60
00:05:12,341 --> 00:05:21,300
In the ancient world thousands of years ago, three very important achievements of the wheel

61
00:05:21,301 --> 00:05:24,660
profoundly and dramatically changed the history of civilization.

62
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:31,540
First, the grinding stone, which ground large quantities of grain more efficiently than

63
00:05:31,541 --> 00:05:33,440
an individual with a mortar and pestle.

64
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:38,860
And both millstones have a groove pattern cut into their faces.

65
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:43,800
The stone above it, not quite touching it, just a little bit above it, rotates.

66
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:47,040
The grain falls in the hole in the middle of the top stone.

67
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:49,440
The grain works its way out along those grooves,

68
00:05:49,620 --> 00:05:53,560
and as the top stone rotates, the grooves cross like scissors and shear apart the grain.

69
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:01,440
Entire communities could form around a single large grinding stone

70
00:06:01,441 --> 00:06:05,660
that might be powered by harnessing cattle rather than human power.

71
00:06:05,661 --> 00:06:10,640
grinding stones were carved out of large rocks

72
00:06:10,641 --> 00:06:13,980
then chiseled into round flat grooved discs

73
00:06:13,981 --> 00:06:19,660
now that grain could be processed in such large quantities

74
00:06:19,661 --> 00:06:21,420
storage became a concern

75
00:06:21,421 --> 00:06:24,800
but the wheels solved this problem too

76
00:06:24,801 --> 00:06:29,340
potter's wheels used for making clay pots

77
00:06:29,341 --> 00:06:31,800
were initially created during the bronze age

78
00:06:31,801 --> 00:06:36,440
then later refined by the Egyptians roughly 5,000 years ago.

79
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:45,920
Potters could throw thin-walled pots with great storage capacity

80
00:06:45,921 --> 00:06:48,760
in a fraction of the time it took to make a pot without a wheel.

81
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:53,240
Pottery wheels are still used today.

82
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:58,100
With a kick wheel, you have the wheel head, which is where the potter puts the clay.

83
00:06:58,101 --> 00:07:03,460
Below that, there's a shaft, which is connected to a flywheel at the very base.

84
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:06,480
And the flywheel has quite a bit of weight to it,

85
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:09,420
so that the momentum will keep the wheel turning

86
00:07:09,421 --> 00:07:12,400
as the potter's kicking and working and alternating.

87
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:17,080
A flywheel is a wheel with added weight,

88
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:22,040
usually placed at its circumference to store kinetic energy, or the energy of motion.

89
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:27,540
Basically what it means is the faster a mass is moving,

90
00:07:27,541 --> 00:07:28,720
the more energy it has.

91
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:30,820
And you know, if you've got a wheel that's spinning

92
00:07:30,821 --> 00:07:33,620
right towards the center, things aren't moving very fast.

93
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:35,780
But towards the outside, for the same amount of road,

94
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:37,140
you're moving really fast.

95
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:40,320
So if you put your mass on the outside of the flywheel,

96
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:44,440
it's moving faster, it carries more kinetic energy,

97
00:07:45,020 --> 00:07:47,320
and therefore it's storing more energy for future use.

98
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:52,620
The invention of the flywheel would have a wide-ranging impact

99
00:07:52,621 --> 00:07:54,360
far beyond the Humble Potter's Wheel,

100
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:56,140
which would benefit from improvements

101
00:07:56,141 --> 00:07:57,780
that included the treadle.

102
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:03,040
In addition to the potter's wheel and the grinding stone,

103
00:08:03,460 --> 00:08:05,040
the third application of the wheel,

104
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:08,320
and possibly most significant, was for transportation.

105
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:12,780
Early log wheels evolved into narrow carved wheels

106
00:08:12,781 --> 00:08:14,460
that could be connected by an axle.

107
00:08:15,620 --> 00:08:17,940
An axle is a structure that is connected to,

108
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:19,620
yet independent from, a wheel.

109
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:24,200
It is often found at the center of a wheel's rotation, or axis.

110
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:33,140
Wheeled carts helped transport goods, for instance, excess grain stored in clay pots.

111
00:08:33,141 --> 00:08:39,140
The Sumerians provide the first archaeological evidence of wheeled vehicles.

112
00:08:39,141 --> 00:08:44,140
Their chariots pulled by oxen date from as early as 3000 BC.

113
00:08:44,141 --> 00:08:50,140
Well, the craft of building wheels, especially in wagons and carriages, to me is very fascinating

114
00:08:50,141 --> 00:08:55,140
because the art of making the wheel, of course, ultimately creates a specialty called the wheelwright.

115
00:08:55,141 --> 00:08:56,239
to wheelwright.

116
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:01,880
Early wheelwrights made wheels by carving rims that would be clamped together

117
00:09:01,881 --> 00:09:06,840
with cross pieces, with an axle fitted into the hub or center.

118
00:09:06,841 --> 00:09:08,680
A leather bearing might

119
00:09:08,681 --> 00:09:11,916
be inserted into the hub to protect the axle.

120
00:09:11,917 --> 00:09:15,080
The thing that's most useful to me, I think,

121
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:19,560
in considering the history of the wheel, is that the wheel is only a part of a system.

122
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:23,080
A wheel's no good unless you have a good road for it to go on.

123
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:27,020
With the onset of carts, chariots, and wagons,

124
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:29,320
roads took on greater importance.

125
00:09:30,060 --> 00:09:31,240
Trade routes were established,

126
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:33,560
and civilizations could more readily expand.

127
00:09:34,060 --> 00:09:36,060
But the wheel did much more than encourage

128
00:09:36,061 --> 00:09:37,740
the creation of carts and roads.

129
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:41,480
It made migration easier,

130
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:44,440
and at relatively slight cost.

131
00:09:44,441 --> 00:09:47,540
It meant that you could move without being nomadic.

132
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:49,980
You could bring along with you

133
00:09:49,981 --> 00:09:52,440
what in Roman times was called the impedimenta,

134
00:09:52,441 --> 00:09:58,280
This was the spouse, the children, the relevant family possessions, etc.

135
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:02,540
It meant, therefore, that you could move your culture to another place.

136
00:10:04,100 --> 00:10:09,480
Before long, wheeled devices for transportation were available to farmers tilling the soil,

137
00:10:10,020 --> 00:10:13,620
traders transporting goods, and armies transporting soldiers.

138
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:22,180
The Roman road would ultimately be based upon the 15-foot width of two heavy carts,

139
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:25,780
or two columns of troops side by side.

140
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:29,860
Once you've got that basic idea of a wheel,

141
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:34,980
then people are very quickly going to figure out all the fascinating applications,

142
00:10:34,981 --> 00:10:37,980
whether two-wheeled carts pulled by other human beings,

143
00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:41,820
or animals, or wheelbarrows, or chariots, or whatever.

144
00:10:42,900 --> 00:10:47,120
The wheel changed our sense of location,

145
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:51,420
our sense of rootedness.

146
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:56,980
it must have had a chilling effect once these implications dawned on us.

147
00:10:57,020 --> 00:10:59,200
To some extent, it still has a chilling effect.

148
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:01,540
It does give rise to the question,

149
00:11:01,541 --> 00:11:07,120
well, if I am not meant to be here, now and abidingly,

150
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:12,420
is there any sense associated with my being meant to be?

151
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:14,980
Or is everything in flux?

152
00:11:16,180 --> 00:11:19,620
The wheel really is the device that says

153
00:11:19,621 --> 00:11:23,700
no one is ever occupying precisely the same position twice

154
00:11:23,701 --> 00:11:25,720
once this thing starts rolling.

155
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:31,920
Next, wheels rolled across the oceans as vital components of ships.

156
00:11:32,660 --> 00:11:36,460
And long-distance trade made the importance of accurate timetables

157
00:11:36,461 --> 00:11:39,940
a new challenge that the promise of wheels would solve.

158
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:47,080
Although humans began settling in villages approximately 10,000 years ago,

159
00:11:47,081 --> 00:11:52,040
It would be another 5,000 years before archaeological evidence of wheels would appear.

160
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:07,600
During the late Middle Ages, as trade increased partly due to the application of wheels,

161
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:12,200
merchants looked across the seas to new markets for buying and selling goods.

162
00:12:12,201 --> 00:12:19,200
Large ships were built, using steering wheels to maneuver the rudder and direct the ship.

163
00:12:19,201 --> 00:12:25,200
As the boat gets bigger, a tiller doesn't give you enough advantage, mechanical advantage

164
00:12:25,201 --> 00:12:30,200
on the rudder, so you may hook up some gears and shafts or belts and pulleys and ropes,

165
00:12:30,201 --> 00:12:34,200
but then you'll have a wheel because it's just so convenient to be able to stand in one spot

166
00:12:34,201 --> 00:12:37,200
and control the boat.

167
00:12:37,201 --> 00:12:44,200
Cylindrical wheels, called windlasses, took up or let out rope to control the sails and anchor.

168
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:56,200
The application of specific wheels often met specific challenges.

169
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:03,200
But the concept of wheels, wheel hood so to speak, seemed endlessly recyclable.

170
00:13:03,201 --> 00:13:15,921
Perhaps the evolution, the changes, the inventions associated with the wheel somehow reflect something very fundamental about us as humans, about how our brains work,

171
00:13:15,922 --> 00:13:18,680
about how society evolves over time.

172
00:13:18,681 --> 00:13:24,840
And the factors, the influences, the technologies that change who we are and what we do.

173
00:13:27,160 --> 00:13:29,760
Not all cultures, however, have made use of wheels.

174
00:13:30,660 --> 00:13:32,840
Despite its technological proficiency,

175
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:36,280
the Middle East eventually abandoned the wheel for transport

176
00:13:36,281 --> 00:13:38,780
because the camel better met its needs.

177
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:42,680
So the wheel is not the only solution to every problem.

178
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:46,620
Today we think the wheel is real important.

179
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:50,720
And it's important to remember that that's not always been true.

180
00:13:50,880 --> 00:13:54,940
The whole notion that the wheel and fire are the two most important inventions

181
00:13:54,941 --> 00:13:56,520
is a pretty recent idea.

182
00:13:56,521 --> 00:14:00,860
Although the wheel is often not the only solution,

183
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:05,380
it is frequently a tangible framework for seemingly intangible challenges,

184
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:07,880
such as the regulation of time.

185
00:14:09,100 --> 00:14:12,880
We think of the sun as a form of timekeeping in itself.

186
00:14:14,340 --> 00:14:18,040
Rising in the morning and setting in the evening

187
00:14:18,041 --> 00:14:22,720
instantly gives a human being a sense of rhythm and form to time.

188
00:14:22,721 --> 00:14:28,700
And so using the sun was an early means of timekeeping by using a sundial.

189
00:14:30,180 --> 00:14:33,060
But sundials aren't precision instruments.

190
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:40,560
As trade and travel increased, the need for accurate and reliable timetables increased with it.

191
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:45,340
Early clocks lacked the necessary accuracy.

192
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:52,220
What was needed and created around the 13th century was the modern wheelworks clock.

193
00:14:52,720 --> 00:15:01,260
Most people, when they think about a clock, immediately they look at the face of the clock, with the hands and all the rest of it.

194
00:15:01,920 --> 00:15:06,700
That's almost, from my point of view, the wrong end of the clock.

195
00:15:06,701 --> 00:15:11,160
If it's a pendulum clock that we're talking about, the pendulum, in fact, is the timekeeper.

196
00:15:14,740 --> 00:15:19,940
Weights suspended from a fixed distance oscillate or swing at the same rate,

197
00:15:19,941 --> 00:15:23,700
no matter how heavy the weight, nor how wide the swing.

198
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:29,880
Once the pendulum is swinging at a fixed rate, say 60 times per minute,

199
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:32,200
the clock is keeping time.

200
00:15:33,740 --> 00:15:37,500
A device called an escapement lowers a weight in discrete measures

201
00:15:37,501 --> 00:15:41,220
using specially designed wheels called gears.

202
00:15:43,480 --> 00:15:46,580
The gears help swing the pendulum and count each swing

203
00:15:46,581 --> 00:15:49,780
through the application of teeth carved into the gear.

204
00:15:49,940 --> 00:15:52,400
to correspond with the teeth of another gear.

205
00:15:57,120 --> 00:16:01,400
Gears are really nothing more than a set of wheels in some predetermined ratio,

206
00:16:01,720 --> 00:16:05,480
and you put the gear teeth on to be sure that it doesn't slip, but they're wheels nonetheless.

207
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:11,320
These teeth turned the wheel into a series of levers extending from the hub.

208
00:16:12,020 --> 00:16:16,160
19th century observers even referred to the wheel as the perpetual lever.

209
00:16:16,161 --> 00:16:26,040
In fact, Galileo, in analyzing the simple machines in the 1500s,

210
00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:33,380
broke down all of the simple machines into a combination or a form of lever.

211
00:16:34,920 --> 00:16:39,480
As clockmaking was refined, gears were used to create minute and second hands,

212
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,200
greatly increasing the accuracy of the clocks.

213
00:16:42,201 --> 00:16:51,200
Gears were made small to fit into wrist watches or large to operate clocks in church steeples.

214
00:16:51,201 --> 00:17:00,960
They were even used to trigger chimes using a mallet connected to the gear system or gear

215
00:17:00,961 --> 00:17:03,113
train that struck a bell.

216
00:17:03,114 --> 00:17:08,280
The principle of accurate timekeeping was applied on a large

217
00:17:08,281 --> 00:17:13,992
scale to help navigation. The Earth was mapped out in a series of grids.

218
00:17:13,993 --> 00:17:15,420
Each grid measured

219
00:17:15,421 --> 00:17:20,120
the distance the sun's light traveled across the surface of the Earth in four-minute increments,

220
00:17:20,520 --> 00:17:21,500
or longitude.

221
00:17:21,501 --> 00:17:27,000
With an accurate clock and a simple equation, a sailor could now pinpoint

222
00:17:27,001 --> 00:17:29,950
his position anywhere on the planet.

223
00:17:29,951 --> 00:17:34,620
Longitude only works if the Earth rotates in a wheel-like

224
00:17:34,621 --> 00:17:38,574
fashion. Now what would be a more perfect wheel?

225
00:17:38,575 --> 00:17:42,280
Well one more perfect wheel is the wheel-like

226
00:17:42,281 --> 00:17:49,700
nature of the heavens themselves where we see the axle essentially a gravitational field and where

227
00:17:49,701 --> 00:17:56,620
we see a wheel now a planet thus connected to other planets going around in a certain quasi

228
00:17:56,621 --> 00:17:58,620
wheel-like fashion.

229
00:17:58,621 --> 00:18:05,880
And in fact, when astronomers wanted to make tiny models of the solar system, they used

230
00:18:05,881 --> 00:18:10,860
a series of wheels and a device called an ori, in which the planets were on wheels that

231
00:18:10,861 --> 00:18:16,100
moved around a central sphere which represented the sun.

232
00:18:16,101 --> 00:18:22,000
There's this wonderful progression from the physical reality of a wheel to the metaphor

233
00:18:22,001 --> 00:18:26,360
of a wheel in space as planets orbit the sun or as the moon orbits the earth.

234
00:18:27,440 --> 00:18:30,240
As wheels provide transport to different worlds,

235
00:18:30,600 --> 00:18:32,980
they transport themselves into different applications.

236
00:18:33,960 --> 00:18:36,720
Technology incorporated the wheel into the printing press,

237
00:18:37,300 --> 00:18:42,100
facilitating mass-produced communication by pressing an inked plate onto paper

238
00:18:42,101 --> 00:18:43,380
attached to a roller.

239
00:18:44,680 --> 00:18:47,080
Wheels were used to give the press constant pressure

240
00:18:47,081 --> 00:18:49,580
and to speed up the printing process.

241
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:53,180
Although the wheel wasn't essential to the production of books,

242
00:18:53,640 --> 00:18:55,080
it made the process more efficient.

243
00:18:56,860 --> 00:19:00,820
What the book does is it puts thought in a form

244
00:19:00,821 --> 00:19:02,620
that allows it to be moved on wheels.

245
00:19:02,780 --> 00:19:07,100
It allows one to speak to the world as if the world were a person.

246
00:19:07,340 --> 00:19:12,300
And it thus contains within it the possibility of revolutionizing the world

247
00:19:12,301 --> 00:19:14,040
without a drop of blood being shed.

248
00:19:14,100 --> 00:19:16,360
Not to say that much blood will not be shed,

249
00:19:16,361 --> 00:19:20,660
and in fact much blood will be shed because of ideas that are presented in books.

250
00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:29,000
But it does mean that you now are capable of persuading without coercing, and in numbers.

251
00:19:31,300 --> 00:19:38,740
From the esoteric to the earthy, agrarian uses for wheels eventually included early windmills,

252
00:19:38,980 --> 00:19:41,040
which arrived in Europe around the 12th century.

253
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:47,520
Windmills captured wind using large blades, often covered with cloth, connected to a hub.

254
00:19:48,120 --> 00:19:51,900
The hub was attached to an axle that led to a series of gears.

255
00:19:54,520 --> 00:19:59,040
The windmill could pivot on wheeled rollers to adjust to changing wind directions.

256
00:20:02,920 --> 00:20:08,760
Windmills vary from traditional Dutch windmills to the familiar prairie windmills of the American plains.

257
00:20:11,040 --> 00:20:15,840
to the giant turbines that generate electricity on wind farms.

258
00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:27,380
Using gear trains, windmills pumped water out of wells, drained swamplands, and even ground wheat.

259
00:20:29,220 --> 00:20:35,240
As this 300-year-old Cape Cod windmill, the oldest in the country, once did for the pilgrims.

260
00:20:35,241 --> 00:20:45,880
What's interesting about the windmill, however, is that it harnesses naturally occurring energy

261
00:20:45,881 --> 00:20:48,300
rather than human or animal power.

262
00:20:49,020 --> 00:20:54,100
One of the amazing things about wheels is the ability to take energy sources from around

263
00:20:54,101 --> 00:20:58,740
us, just the environment, and convert them to useful mechanical energy using a wheel.

264
00:20:59,700 --> 00:21:04,980
Next, the concept of harnessing natural power would spark the Industrial Revolution.

265
00:21:05,240 --> 00:21:08,240
and create even more ways to utilize wheels.

266
00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:16,240
More than 100 million bicycles are manufactured worldwide each year,

267
00:21:16,241 --> 00:21:21,240
with a bicycle being a primary mode of transportation in many developing countries.

268
00:21:21,241 --> 00:21:35,241
The mills of Europe, and later America, changed the natural environment into a resource to

269
00:21:36,620 --> 00:21:38,934
be exploited by the wheel.

270
00:21:38,935 --> 00:21:44,720
Nature became another tool for human progress, but the concept of

271
00:21:44,721 --> 00:21:49,060
harnessing wind and water actually dates back to the second century B.C.

272
00:21:51,460 --> 00:21:58,840
Earliest uses that we know of are water lifting for irrigation and grinding grain,

273
00:21:59,140 --> 00:22:00,460
grinding wheat to flour.

274
00:22:02,520 --> 00:22:05,760
Water wheels harness water power in different ways.

275
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:11,040
One is an impact water wheel, commonly called an undershot water wheel,

276
00:22:11,041 --> 00:22:13,120
where the water strikes a flat paddle

277
00:22:13,121 --> 00:22:15,860
and the impact causes the wheel to rotate.

278
00:22:16,720 --> 00:22:19,820
A second type of water wheel is called an overshot

279
00:22:19,821 --> 00:22:21,500
or a weight-driven water wheel

280
00:22:21,501 --> 00:22:23,720
where the water comes over the top of the wheel,

281
00:22:24,040 --> 00:22:25,240
descends into a bucket,

282
00:22:25,480 --> 00:22:28,020
and the weight of the water in the buckets

283
00:22:28,021 --> 00:22:30,480
on one side of the wheel causes it to turn.

284
00:22:31,540 --> 00:22:33,300
At the beginning of the 12th century,

285
00:22:33,580 --> 00:22:36,180
England had more than 5,000 mills,

286
00:22:36,560 --> 00:22:38,600
each one a community power plant

287
00:22:38,601 --> 00:22:40,860
for a host of otherwise burdensome tasks

288
00:22:40,861 --> 00:22:43,860
far beyond just lifting water and grinding grain.

289
00:22:45,120 --> 00:22:49,680
European technicians and, concurrently, Chinese technicians

290
00:22:49,681 --> 00:22:53,100
diversify the use of water power to whereby 1,500,

291
00:22:53,400 --> 00:22:57,580
they know how to use water power for pounding metals,

292
00:22:57,820 --> 00:23:00,560
to shape metals for pounding ore prior to reduction,

293
00:23:01,020 --> 00:23:04,340
for grinding a whole host of things other than wheat.

294
00:23:06,180 --> 00:23:09,540
Rivers, abundant in the northeastern United States,

295
00:23:09,541 --> 00:23:11,780
would provide an accessible source of power

296
00:23:11,781 --> 00:23:13,480
during the early days of the nation.

297
00:23:15,260 --> 00:23:17,800
There are a whole lot of rivers and streams

298
00:23:17,801 --> 00:23:20,060
that drop a fairly great distance,

299
00:23:20,220 --> 00:23:22,260
and the Industrial Revolution in America

300
00:23:22,261 --> 00:23:24,640
really takes off based on water power,

301
00:23:25,220 --> 00:23:27,520
particularly in the countryside.

302
00:23:29,100 --> 00:23:31,320
Villages and towns dug canals

303
00:23:31,321 --> 00:23:34,200
and built mills to saw wood,

304
00:23:35,280 --> 00:23:35,980
spin wool,

305
00:23:36,780 --> 00:23:37,880
and grind grain.

306
00:23:39,540 --> 00:23:44,920
On top of the millstones, you have a hopper, a big wooden funnel that holds whole dried grain,

307
00:23:46,220 --> 00:23:48,100
be it wheat, rye, corn, barley.

308
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:57,060
And below that, there's a shoe, a little wooden trough that directs the grain from the hopper into a pair of millstones.

309
00:23:57,780 --> 00:24:04,640
There's a vertical wooden shaft called a damsel that rotates with the millstone and shakes the shoe.

310
00:24:04,641 --> 00:24:13,340
This process is possible because the water wheel is connected through an elaborate system

311
00:24:13,341 --> 00:24:18,220
of gears and flywheels to the millstone, or whatever the mill was assigned to do.

312
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:26,880
It represents a sort of quantum leap in the amount of energy that humans were able to extract.

313
00:24:27,380 --> 00:24:33,140
A human being can produce about one-tenth of a horsepower continuously over a six- or eight-hour day.

314
00:24:33,141 --> 00:24:44,020
That means even a small, two-horsepower water wheel is producing 20 times as much energy as a human being, and it doesn't get tired.

315
00:24:48,280 --> 00:24:57,560
Water mills are an ingenious use of available resources, and by simply reversing the concept of the water wheel, you end up with something entirely different.

316
00:24:57,561 --> 00:25:03,560
One of the neat things about wheels is the way you can turn this energy conversion around.

317
00:25:03,561 --> 00:25:07,680
We have water wheels that produce energy of all sorts, whether it's electrical energy

318
00:25:07,681 --> 00:25:13,340
or energy for a gristmill, but put it on a paddle boat, connect it to a steam engine,

319
00:25:13,341 --> 00:25:15,860
and you've got a paddle steamer.

320
00:25:15,861 --> 00:25:18,060
So it works both ways.

321
00:25:18,061 --> 00:25:22,540
The paddle wheel of a paddle steamer can be thought of as a series of ores attached

322
00:25:22,541 --> 00:25:25,040
to a wheel.

323
00:25:25,041 --> 00:25:29,240
Robert Fulton's paddle boats began flying the Mississippi in the early 19th century.

324
00:25:30,120 --> 00:25:35,940
But the business end of a paddle steamer is really the steam engine that generates the energy to move the paddles.

325
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:43,440
And here again, the concept of the wheel manifests itself in an aptly named mechanism called the sun and planet gear.

326
00:25:44,420 --> 00:25:50,520
The planet is a small gear that orbits the sun, or gear connected by an axle to a driving wheel.

327
00:25:50,521 --> 00:25:57,900
As steam is forced into a piston chamber, it expands and moves a lever attached to the

328
00:25:57,901 --> 00:26:04,040
planet wheel, which is in turn connected to a spoke coming from the sun.

329
00:26:04,041 --> 00:26:09,840
The sun then moves the driving gear.

330
00:26:09,841 --> 00:26:13,880
The principle of the sun and planet gear is well illustrated on another transportation

331
00:26:13,881 --> 00:26:18,320
device that revolutionized the world, the steam locomotive.

332
00:26:18,321 --> 00:26:31,140
At certain times in American mechanical history, rotary devices seem to be the way to solve all problems.

333
00:26:31,580 --> 00:26:38,300
So for a while, in the 1840s in particular, you have all these very, very clever rotary steam engines.

334
00:26:39,540 --> 00:26:43,740
Trains obliterated frontiers in America in the mid-19th century.

335
00:26:43,741 --> 00:26:46,500
The entire country was crisscrossed with track.

336
00:26:46,501 --> 00:26:51,020
There were companies that manufactured car wheels,

337
00:26:51,200 --> 00:26:54,320
usually the axle and the two wheels to go into railway cars,

338
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:57,680
but locomotive wheels would be built right along with the rest of the locomotives,

339
00:26:57,840 --> 00:27:00,920
heavy castings, and then turned on a heavy lathe.

340
00:27:01,780 --> 00:27:05,020
And usually the wheel itself would be cast steel,

341
00:27:05,260 --> 00:27:07,800
and then there'd be a forged steel tire,

342
00:27:07,801 --> 00:27:10,620
and the tire would be expanded with a little heat,

343
00:27:10,780 --> 00:27:14,280
and then put in place, and then allowed to cool and shrink down tight.

344
00:27:14,281 --> 00:27:25,943
With the advent of trains, wheat could be grown in Kansas, shipped to Chicago, and be ground, then packaged and distributed to New York or California, all mobilized on

345
00:27:25,944 --> 00:27:27,060
railroad wheels.

346
00:27:29,120 --> 00:27:38,360
As railroads regulated the nation, they helped grease the Industrial Revolution, providing markets for goods more efficiently produced because of the wheel.

347
00:27:42,249 --> 00:27:52,477
The part of the wheel in the Industrial Revolution is central, not only as it influenced factory production, but in fact as it made possible a steam locomotive and a

348
00:27:52,478 --> 00:28:02,520
veritable empire that will depend on the movement of troops, the movement of people, the crossing of entire continents in periods of time heretofore unimaginable.

349
00:28:02,521 --> 00:28:09,520
The Industrial Revolution, in fact, rolls inexorably on wheels.

350
00:28:09,521 --> 00:28:18,520
Next, by the turn of the 20th century, this inexorable roll would generate even more wheel-based

351
00:28:18,521 --> 00:28:23,520
innovations.

352
00:28:23,521 --> 00:28:30,520
The number of locomotives in the United States peaked at nearly 70,000 in the early 1920s.

353
00:28:30,521 --> 00:28:42,340
As wheels harness natural power, they liberate us from otherwise exhausting or impossible tasks.

354
00:28:47,060 --> 00:28:52,980
But the idea of the wheel as liberator took an ironic turn in places like Lowell, Massachusetts,

355
00:28:53,280 --> 00:28:54,760
during the mid-19th century.

356
00:28:55,760 --> 00:29:00,160
With its textile mills full of machines operated by young farm girls,

357
00:29:00,520 --> 00:29:09,760
Textiles had for centuries used simple hand-operated spinning wheels to twist cotton, wool, and

358
00:29:09,761 --> 00:29:11,140
flax into thread.

359
00:29:12,820 --> 00:29:17,780
But wheel-based innovations at the turn of the 19th century increased production.

360
00:29:18,880 --> 00:29:21,800
The vertical spinners or jennies came in.

361
00:29:21,801 --> 00:29:26,760
Again, it was an effort to get more production with one worker.

362
00:29:26,761 --> 00:29:30,440
And so there are 10 spindles, all run by one wheel.

363
00:29:32,660 --> 00:29:37,600
Eventually, single machines spun 200 spindles of wool at the same time.

364
00:29:38,620 --> 00:29:42,480
Planned communities like Lowell formed to produce textiles along rivers,

365
00:29:42,620 --> 00:29:44,000
like the Concord and the Merrimack,

366
00:29:44,460 --> 00:29:48,200
which generated roughly 10,000 horsepower to Lowell's water wheels.

367
00:29:49,980 --> 00:29:55,540
It was an interesting social experiment to bring in the farm girls.

368
00:29:55,541 --> 00:30:02,220
And they thought they were being liberated from the hard work of the farms,

369
00:30:02,300 --> 00:30:06,500
only to discover that life in the mills could be long hours,

370
00:30:06,640 --> 00:30:10,100
and that the hours were ruled not by the sun, but by the mill bell.

371
00:30:11,100 --> 00:30:13,940
What the Industrial Revolution did for women

372
00:30:13,941 --> 00:30:21,280
was that it rendered them to some extent independent of marriage.

373
00:30:22,160 --> 00:30:24,700
It made self-support possible.

374
00:30:24,701 --> 00:30:32,120
but by creating an entirely unliberated world of incessant labor,

375
00:30:32,960 --> 00:30:38,980
of discomfort, and the conversion of oneself into a kind of instrument or tool.

376
00:30:40,500 --> 00:30:42,140
A cog, if you will.

377
00:30:45,060 --> 00:30:50,160
The success of our machinery has convinced us, almost to a person,

378
00:30:50,161 --> 00:30:54,440
that there is no problem so great that some machine can't solve it,

379
00:30:54,700 --> 00:30:58,180
And to that extent, we do tend to look for mechanical solutions

380
00:30:58,181 --> 00:31:00,280
to problems that are not mechanical.

381
00:31:01,900 --> 00:31:05,620
But the inexorably spinning wheels of the Industrial Revolution

382
00:31:05,621 --> 00:31:08,340
produced an inexhaustible supply of products,

383
00:31:08,820 --> 00:31:10,540
including more wheels.

384
00:31:13,520 --> 00:31:16,200
Despite our constantly reinventing the wheel,

385
00:31:16,201 --> 00:31:18,960
the concept of the wheel hasn't really evolved,

386
00:31:19,560 --> 00:31:21,520
a testimony to its simple purity.

387
00:31:24,700 --> 00:31:26,980
But in practice, wheels do evolve.

388
00:31:27,480 --> 00:31:29,680
They improve through technology and materials.

389
00:31:30,700 --> 00:31:33,120
The log evolved into the carved wheel,

390
00:31:33,520 --> 00:31:35,480
which became the lighter, more efficient,

391
00:31:35,720 --> 00:31:37,980
hollowed rim with spokes and a hub.

392
00:31:39,500 --> 00:31:41,840
The tire evolved from a metal casing

393
00:31:41,841 --> 00:31:44,340
into something altogether transformative

394
00:31:44,341 --> 00:31:46,460
with the advent of the automobile.

395
00:31:48,160 --> 00:31:51,480
Because of speed and heaviness of a car,

396
00:31:51,720 --> 00:31:53,120
you've got to look differently at a vehicle

397
00:31:53,121 --> 00:31:56,060
that's going, say, 5, 10 miles an hour drawn by a horse

398
00:31:56,061 --> 00:31:58,920
on the rugged roads of the 19th century.

399
00:31:59,840 --> 00:32:02,480
If you've got a car that's capable of 20 miles an hour,

400
00:32:02,940 --> 00:32:05,460
you've increased the stress on the wheels about four times,

401
00:32:06,060 --> 00:32:08,040
and your old wagon wheels begin to fall apart.

402
00:32:09,620 --> 00:32:12,760
The automobile itself was another collection of gears.

403
00:32:13,600 --> 00:32:16,760
Crankshaft and steering wheel, ball bearing and transmission,

404
00:32:17,200 --> 00:32:19,200
all rolled out on an assembly line,

405
00:32:19,500 --> 00:32:21,360
again a variation on the wheel.

406
00:32:23,120 --> 00:32:27,800
But of all the wheels connected to the car, the greatest may be the pneumatic tire.

407
00:32:32,420 --> 00:32:39,060
Tires were originally just cloth or leather coverings wrapped around a wood or metal wheel to provide cushioning.

408
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:41,380
Eventually, hard rubber was used.

409
00:32:42,280 --> 00:32:48,520
Finally, pneumatic tires, which trapped a cushion of air inside the tire, were employed, initially on bicycles.

410
00:32:48,521 --> 00:32:55,520
But by the early 20th century, they found their way onto cars and trucks.

411
00:33:01,520 --> 00:33:08,520
Air is the most efficient thing that will carry the load and give you cushioning and you need certain footprint characteristics,

412
00:33:08,521 --> 00:33:13,520
force and moment characteristics for the tire and the deflection of the tire will provide that

413
00:33:13,521 --> 00:33:19,760
provide that and gives you very even distribution of load across the footprint with just air

414
00:33:19,761 --> 00:33:21,500
in the cavity.

415
00:33:21,501 --> 00:33:28,200
Before air could be pumped into a rubber tire, the rubber had to be hardened, made impervious

416
00:33:28,201 --> 00:33:30,480
to changes in the weather.

417
00:33:30,481 --> 00:33:35,760
In 1839, Charles Goodyear, an inventor, searched for an additive that would keep rubber from

418
00:33:35,761 --> 00:33:38,760
from turning brittle in the cold and sticky in the heat.

419
00:33:45,760 --> 00:33:47,760
He stumbled upon vulcanization,

420
00:33:47,761 --> 00:33:49,760
a curing process that basically cooks rubber

421
00:33:49,761 --> 00:33:52,760
until it stabilizes and holds its shape.

422
00:33:53,760 --> 00:33:55,760
So he had a lot of different chemicals

423
00:33:55,761 --> 00:33:57,760
that he was experimenting with,

424
00:33:57,761 --> 00:34:00,760
and he accidentally spilled some of the sulfur on the stove,

425
00:34:00,761 --> 00:34:04,760
and yet sulfur will melt under those conditions

426
00:34:04,761 --> 00:34:08,660
and he had some rubber laying on the stove and it ran together

427
00:34:08,661 --> 00:34:11,580
and it vulcanized, lo and behold.

428
00:34:12,360 --> 00:34:16,480
By the 1950s, synthetic rubber had replaced natural rubber.

429
00:34:16,880 --> 00:34:20,440
Tire companies like Goodyear really were reinventing the wheel.

430
00:34:21,360 --> 00:34:24,600
Again, the ideal wheel, the round thing that's frictionless,

431
00:34:24,780 --> 00:34:26,460
that's not something that gets better.

432
00:34:26,600 --> 00:34:30,360
But the practical wheel, the wheel that gives better mileage

433
00:34:30,361 --> 00:34:33,100
or breaks better or is lighter,

434
00:34:33,101 --> 00:34:40,260
That's a point where you can spend a lot of money and, over the years, get a lot of improvement in efficiency.

435
00:34:42,580 --> 00:34:46,680
Today, making tires is a complex and highly specialized endeavor.

436
00:34:47,480 --> 00:34:53,700
There are up to 15 different components in today's steel-belted radial tire.

437
00:34:53,701 --> 00:35:06,480
You have the beads, the plies, there's wedges, apexes, liner, sidewall, tread compound, sidewall compound, overlays.

438
00:35:07,040 --> 00:35:15,420
There is some ongoing innovation work with tires with computer chips in them that will read the road, that will help control the vehicle.

439
00:35:15,421 --> 00:35:18,400
They will be an integral part of the vehicle control system,

440
00:35:18,480 --> 00:35:21,120
and they'll be reading it from inside the tire,

441
00:35:21,280 --> 00:35:24,060
which is a great advantage since it's closer to the road.

442
00:35:25,740 --> 00:35:28,740
Goodyear manufactures tires for all sorts of vehicles,

443
00:35:29,380 --> 00:35:31,520
including off-road, earth-moving equipment,

444
00:35:33,120 --> 00:35:34,020
farm vehicles,

445
00:35:34,600 --> 00:35:37,160
and airplanes.

446
00:35:38,220 --> 00:35:41,580
Airplane tires endure particular stress during takeoffs and landings

447
00:35:41,581 --> 00:35:44,580
because of heat caused by the friction from the weight of the cargo.

448
00:35:45,420 --> 00:35:47,880
and the high speeds needed to achieve lift.

449
00:35:50,120 --> 00:35:54,400
Wheels play an indispensable role in the aeronautics industry beyond tires.

450
00:35:55,640 --> 00:35:58,940
The propellers that first lifted the Wright brothers off the ground

451
00:35:58,941 --> 00:36:01,900
use the principle of the windmill, only in reverse.

452
00:36:03,360 --> 00:36:07,440
An axle from the center of the propeller connects through a complex gear system

453
00:36:07,441 --> 00:36:09,600
to an engine that supplies the spin.

454
00:36:10,940 --> 00:36:13,800
Instead of harnessing wind, a propeller generates it,

455
00:36:13,801 --> 00:36:15,620
lifting the plane off the ground.

456
00:36:19,600 --> 00:36:22,060
Switch the propeller from vertical to horizontal,

457
00:36:22,500 --> 00:36:25,480
and the wheel elevates the helicopter's rotary blades

458
00:36:25,481 --> 00:36:29,940
and elevates us from our earthbound existence,

459
00:36:31,080 --> 00:36:33,620
free and unfettered, thanks to wheels.

460
00:36:34,840 --> 00:36:38,200
I think there's a continuum here of mobility, if you will.

461
00:36:38,940 --> 00:36:40,580
And along with that mobility, I think,

462
00:36:40,660 --> 00:36:43,740
comes a lot of the basic freedoms that we, again,

463
00:36:43,800 --> 00:36:48,480
sometimes take for granted the right of free assembly depends on free

464
00:36:48,481 --> 00:36:53,680
transportation, free mobility, at least certainly transportation free of any

465
00:36:53,681 --> 00:37:00,120
political hindrance. Next, the impact of wheels extends far beyond the immediate

466
00:37:00,121 --> 00:37:04,200
benefits we reap. But are there other applications for wheels that we haven't

467
00:37:04,201 --> 00:37:10,400
even conceived of yet? Other materials in which to forge them? Other ways to

468
00:37:10,401 --> 00:37:17,400
experience them.

469
00:37:17,401 --> 00:37:29,400
A typical tire manufacturing plant can produce approximately 25,000 tires in a single day.

470
00:37:29,401 --> 00:37:36,000
If the airplane propeller is a variation on the windmill, then the jet engine is a variation

471
00:37:36,001 --> 00:37:41,060
on the turbine, which was originally a type of water wheel.

472
00:37:41,061 --> 00:37:46,200
Water turbines are essentially water wheels with curved surfaces.

473
00:37:46,201 --> 00:37:53,960
They produce power by water flowing smoothly over a curved surface because the water acts

474
00:37:53,961 --> 00:38:00,280
over their entire surface simultaneously instead of on only one paddle or one bucket at a time.

475
00:38:00,281 --> 00:38:03,760
They produce much more power in a much more compact form.

476
00:38:05,960 --> 00:38:10,620
Hoover Dam makes use of over 17 14-foot-wide turbines

477
00:38:10,621 --> 00:38:14,680
to generate 2,991,000 horsepower,

478
00:38:15,260 --> 00:38:17,880
which is then transformed into electricity.

479
00:38:19,100 --> 00:38:22,540
Moving magnets create electricity.

480
00:38:23,280 --> 00:38:27,560
So in every turbine, you have coils of wire and you have magnets.

481
00:38:27,561 --> 00:38:30,620
and as you turn those coils of wire in the vicinity of a magnetic field,

482
00:38:31,140 --> 00:38:34,280
that's what produces the electricity that we use in our homes.

483
00:38:34,780 --> 00:38:40,160
Now you can generate that circular motion with a water-driven turbine, a water wheel.

484
00:38:40,660 --> 00:38:45,200
You can generate it with steam in a coal-burning power plant or a nuclear power plant.

485
00:38:45,700 --> 00:38:46,960
You can generate it with wind.

486
00:38:50,740 --> 00:38:54,780
Jet engines replace water with air inside the spinning turbine.

487
00:38:54,781 --> 00:39:01,720
and instead of electricity, jet engines generate thrust as the air is pushed out of the engine, lifting the plane.

488
00:39:03,400 --> 00:39:08,940
From the circular motion of a spinning turbine to the spinning of a disk drive running a computer,

489
00:39:09,560 --> 00:39:11,100
we spin our wheels everywhere.

490
00:39:12,060 --> 00:39:17,860
The CD-ROM is similar to the compact disk, which harkens back to the old vinyl record and turntable,

491
00:39:18,300 --> 00:39:20,860
which was a variation on the ancient hurdy-gurdy.

492
00:39:24,780 --> 00:39:29,300
In the future, we may need a microscope to see our wheels.

493
00:39:29,840 --> 00:39:33,440
One of the great frontiers of the wheels has to do with making them small,

494
00:39:34,060 --> 00:39:35,500
making them microscopically small.

495
00:39:35,580 --> 00:39:36,980
This is nanotechnology.

496
00:39:37,320 --> 00:39:40,680
Right now, we can use lasers, we can use various other techniques

497
00:39:40,681 --> 00:39:44,900
to fabricate tiny pieces, including wheels and gears,

498
00:39:45,660 --> 00:39:48,140
that are a hundredth the diameter of a human hair.

499
00:39:48,141 --> 00:39:51,540
You can make gear systems, gear trains on a surface

500
00:39:51,541 --> 00:39:54,680
that you need the highest power microscope just to see them.

501
00:39:54,780 --> 00:39:56,894
Now, these aren't machines yet.

502
00:39:56,895 --> 00:40:00,780
These are just demonstrating the feasibility of doing it.

503
00:40:00,781 --> 00:40:07,780
But imagine a society, imagine an age when we have micromachines that can build other machines.

504
00:40:07,781 --> 00:40:11,780
Micromachines that can do surgery on our bodies.

505
00:40:11,781 --> 00:40:18,780
Micromachines that can fabricate microelectronics, make your computers smaller, make them more powerful.

506
00:40:18,781 --> 00:40:22,460
Wheels invaded every aspect of our lives

507
00:40:22,461 --> 00:40:25,180
with their undeniable utility and practicality.

508
00:40:25,680 --> 00:40:28,240
And perhaps they've made us practical too.

509
00:40:29,040 --> 00:40:31,180
But there's another wheel that's important to us,

510
00:40:31,680 --> 00:40:33,820
a wheel built solely to a muse.

511
00:40:34,580 --> 00:40:37,480
To talk about Ferris wheels and balls and things like that

512
00:40:37,481 --> 00:40:41,420
as pointless, mere games, sort of life's trifles,

513
00:40:42,100 --> 00:40:45,860
I think we grossly underestimate the power and force,

514
00:40:45,861 --> 00:40:49,600
the imaginative potential, the discipline of the mind

515
00:40:49,601 --> 00:40:54,660
wrought by such trivia as Ferris wheels.

516
00:40:55,040 --> 00:40:57,900
After conquering so much of life's work with wheels,

517
00:40:58,340 --> 00:41:01,080
it's reassuring to acknowledge the importance of play

518
00:41:01,081 --> 00:41:03,620
and of the wheels' importance to play.

519
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:06,260
Ferris wheels, despite their frivolity,

520
00:41:06,500 --> 00:41:08,820
are actually complex wheeled mechanisms

521
00:41:08,821 --> 00:41:10,860
requiring precision engineering.

522
00:41:11,600 --> 00:41:13,720
The largest Ferris wheel in the world

523
00:41:13,721 --> 00:41:15,240
is the Millennium Wheel in London.

524
00:41:15,860 --> 00:41:20,720
opened in February of 2000, with a diameter of 443 feet.

525
00:41:23,540 --> 00:41:26,980
But the first true Ferris wheel was a giant as well,

526
00:41:27,460 --> 00:41:30,760
built for Chicago's Columbian Exposition of 1894

527
00:41:30,761 --> 00:41:34,260
by George Washington Gale Ferris,

528
00:41:34,780 --> 00:41:37,140
an American engineer who understood steel.

529
00:41:37,900 --> 00:41:40,200
It was first rejected out of hand.

530
00:41:40,201 --> 00:41:42,860
It's too wild and too improbable.

531
00:41:42,861 --> 00:41:46,140
so nothing else was forthcoming

532
00:41:46,141 --> 00:41:48,740
so at the last minute they got a hold of Mr. Ferris

533
00:41:48,741 --> 00:41:50,820
and said okay we'll go with your idea

534
00:41:50,821 --> 00:41:56,440
Ferris produced a 250 foot diameter steel wheel

535
00:41:56,441 --> 00:42:00,220
that rose 264 feet off the ground at its highest point

536
00:42:00,221 --> 00:42:04,200
the giant axle was a single forged piece of steel

537
00:42:04,201 --> 00:42:07,200
the largest single forging accomplished at that time

538
00:42:07,201 --> 00:42:09,960
two steam engines powered the wheel

539
00:42:09,961 --> 00:42:15,140
that carried over 2,000 passengers on a 40-minute journey through two revolutions.

540
00:42:15,780 --> 00:42:23,800
The first time, you just got over your fear of sitting there hanging on to the seat,

541
00:42:23,880 --> 00:42:26,740
and the second time, you got to look out and enjoy the ride.

542
00:42:28,360 --> 00:42:33,240
One enthusiastic customer was Lee Sullivan's grandfather, William E. Sullivan,

543
00:42:33,241 --> 00:42:37,700
who talked the wheel's operator into letting him observe the mechanism that drove the wheel.

544
00:42:38,580 --> 00:42:42,760
When he came home, he told Grandma that that's what he wanted to do, build wheels.

545
00:42:43,980 --> 00:42:47,000
She said, well, don't tell anybody. They'll think you've lost your mind.

546
00:42:48,700 --> 00:42:53,880
Lee's grandfather turned his Jacksonville, Illinois bridge-building business into a wheel-making one.

547
00:42:55,160 --> 00:43:00,860
More than 100 years later, it continues as the oldest Ferris wheel manufacturer in the country.

548
00:43:00,861 --> 00:43:09,480
It's a long journey from the grinding stone to the ferris wheel

549
00:43:09,481 --> 00:43:16,820
But the story of wheels is an endless variation on a simple theme

550
00:43:16,821 --> 00:43:24,560
And so our inventions change us

551
00:43:24,561 --> 00:43:26,680
They don't just make us more powerful

552
00:43:26,681 --> 00:43:28,600
They make us think differently

553
00:43:28,601 --> 00:43:30,760
About the same sorts of things

554
00:43:30,761 --> 00:43:34,400
They make us think differently about the role of thought itself.

555
00:43:35,060 --> 00:43:38,000
You think about how much the wheel has driven society,

556
00:43:38,440 --> 00:43:42,680
how much it's increased a single human's ability to do more work,

557
00:43:43,180 --> 00:43:46,000
to get places that humans could never get before,

558
00:43:46,080 --> 00:43:47,360
to travel greater distances,

559
00:43:48,020 --> 00:43:50,460
to do technologies you never could do before,

560
00:43:50,560 --> 00:43:52,920
just the ideas of things you could do with gear systems,

561
00:43:53,460 --> 00:43:54,740
things you could do with water wheels,

562
00:43:55,100 --> 00:43:56,440
things you could do with transportation.

563
00:43:57,580 --> 00:43:59,680
It's transformed society in amazing ways.

564
00:43:59,681 --> 00:44:03,040
It may not be the most important invention ever, but it's certainly right up there.

